          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly avows her love for Lear and presents that feeling as true. The qualification limits its extent to what her filial bond warrants; it does not remove the truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The occurrence explicitly invokes Cordelia’s existing filial bond and affirms that her love conforms to its obligations. Her subsequent explanation identifies those obligations as returning the fitting duties of obedience, love, and honour. This is not a new promise with future terms, but the target, as qualified by the same sentence, positions her as bound by and presently affirming that relationship; the later explanation is interpretive confirmation rather than an unrelated promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"Although Cordelia is distressed by her inability to verbalize her heart, the focal expression is a controlled, carefully delimited avowal rather than a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.85,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and affirming a bond, the expression functions moderately as a corrective refusal of Lear’s demand for competitive, maximized praise. Its qualification rejects exaggeration and limits Cordelia’s answer to what she regards as truthful and properly filial.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Nothing will come of nothing: speak again.\\n\\nCORDELIA.\\nUnhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to declare which loves him most, linking their answers to divisions of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives the focal avowal but restricts it to the love owed by her filial bond rather than competing in her sisters’ hyperbole.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes and affirms an existing filial undertaking: to return the duties fittingly owed to the father who begot, raised, and loved her, specifically including obedience, love, and honour. She does not make a new unlimited promise and expressly limits the bond by “no more nor less,” anticipating that marriage would divide her care and duty. Lear does not accept this as a satisfactory answer; he tells her to amend her speech lest she damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognized as the opening court scene of William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied target and source blocks.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents Cordelia’s affection and her understanding of filial obligation, but it cannot independently establish an inaccessible inner state with certainty. The precise practical terms of her bond beyond the duties she names are not fully specified, and no omitted later events are used to assess this occurrence.\"}"
